{
  "dom_id": "step-e18860",
  "name": "Example: Solving for Two Coefficients",
  "problem": "The equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + 12 = 7x^2 + 2x + 12\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $a$ and $b$  are constants. What is the value of $a + b?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a,$ and on the right-hand side it is $7.$ So, we have \n\n$$\na = 7.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b,$ and on the right-hand side it is $2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nb = 2.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =7+2 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
